---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { aff } from "../data/affiliates";
import { asset, path } from "../lib/paths";
const pageImage = asset("images/hero.jpg");

const faq = [
  {
    q: "Do I need a visa for Georgia?",
    a: "Most travelers don't. Citizens of the EU, UK, US, Canada, Australia, Japan and many other countries can stay visa-free for up to one year. Others may get a visa-free stay if they hold a valid visa or residence permit of certain countries, or can apply for an e-visa at evisa.gov.ge.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is travel insurance mandatory for Georgia in 2026?",
    a: "Yes. Since 1 January 2026 foreign visitors must have health and accident insurance for their whole stay with cover of at least 30,000 GEL (about $11,500). Visa-free travelers are not exempt. The policy can be from any Georgian or foreign insurer and in English, on paper or on your phone.",
  },
  {
    q: "Will they actually check my insurance?",
    a: "Border officers may ask for it at air, land and sea crossings. Checks are not done on every passenger, but airlines may also ask at check-in. Keep the policy ready on your phone and as a PDF offline.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long must my passport be valid?",
    a: "For most nationalities the passport must be valid for your whole stay. Citizens of some countries need a passport valid for at least a year on arrival. Check the official consular site (geoconsul.gov.ge) for your nationality.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I enter Georgia with an EU ID card?",
    a: "Citizens of some countries, including many EU member states, can enter with a national ID card instead of a passport. Your airline may still ask for a passport if you transit another country.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I travel to Abkhazia or South Ossetia?",
    a: "Georgia treats these regions as occupied territories. Entering them from Russia is illegal under Georgian law and can lead to a fine or criminal case, and many governments advise against all travel there.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Georgia Visa & Entry Requirements 2026: Visa-Free Stay, Mandatory Insurance | ArriveGE"
  description="Do you need a visa for Georgia? Who gets 1 year visa-free, the new mandatory travel insurance rule (30,000 GEL cover since 2026), passport validity, e-visa and border tips."
  nextHref="/money-in-georgia"
  nextLabel="money and currency"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqSchema} />

  <header class="page-hero has-photo" style={`--page-image: url('${pageImage}')`}>
    <div class="page-hero-inner" data-reveal>
      <p class="section-kicker">Before you fly</p>
      <h1>Georgia Visa &amp; Entry Requirements</h1>
      <p>
        Most visitors need no visa and can stay up to a year. What changed in 2026: every foreign visitor must have travel
        health insurance for the whole trip.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="section content-grid">
    <article class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2>Quick answer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Visa:</strong> not needed for EU, UK, US, Canada, Australia, Japan and many more — up to 1 year visa-free.</li>
        <li><strong>Insurance:</strong> mandatory since 1 January 2026, minimum 30,000 GEL cover for medical care and accidents.</li>
        <li><strong>Passport:</strong> valid for your whole stay (some nationalities need 1 year left).</li>
        <li><strong>No visa-free deal for you?</strong> Check whether your US, UK, EU or other visa qualifies you, or apply for an e-visa.</li>
        <li><strong>Always confirm</strong> on the official consular site for your passport before you book.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Rules last checked: November 2026.</em></p>

      <h2>Who can enter visa-free</h2>
      <p>
        Georgia has one of the most open visa policies in the region. Citizens of roughly 95 countries — including all EU
        states, the UK, the US, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Japan, South Korea, Israel, the Gulf states and most of
        Latin America — can stay without a visa for up to one year. Some countries get shorter visa-free periods.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your passport is not on the visa-free list, you may still enter visa-free for a limited time if you hold a valid
        visa or residence permit from certain countries (for example the US, the UK or a Schengen state). Rules differ by
        nationality, so check the official list before you buy tickets.
      </p>

      <h2>Mandatory travel insurance (new in 2026)</h2>
      <p>Since 1 January 2026 every foreign visitor needs an insurance policy that:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>covers medical treatment, hospital stays and accidents;</li>
        <li>has a limit of at least <strong>30,000 GEL</strong> (about $11,500 or €10,500);</li>
        <li>is valid for the whole stay, including the days you arrive and leave;</li>
        <li>names Georgia as a covered country;</li>
        <li>is written in English or Georgian, on paper or electronic.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Any insurer is accepted, Georgian or foreign. A normal international travel policy usually qualifies if Georgia is
        included and the medical limit is high enough — check the PDF before you fly. Diplomats, holders of service
        passports and professional drivers are among the few exemptions; children are not exempt.
      </p>
      <p>
        Checks are random rather than universal, but you can be asked at the border and airlines sometimes check at
        check-in. Buying a policy at home is usually cheaper than at the border.
      </p>

      <h2>E-visa</h2>
      <p>
        Travelers who do need a visa can apply online at <strong>evisa.gov.ge</strong>. Apply at least a week before you
        travel; decisions usually come within a few working days. The e-visa allows a shorter stay than visa-free entry.
      </p>

      <h2>What to have ready at the border</h2>
      <table class="compare">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Document</th>
            <th>Needed?</th>
            <th>Tip</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Passport (or eligible ID card)</td>
            <td>Always</td>
            <td>Valid for the whole stay</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Travel insurance</td>
            <td>Yes, since 2026</td>
            <td>Save the PDF offline on your phone</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Return or onward ticket</td>
            <td>Sometimes asked</td>
            <td>Keep the booking handy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Hotel booking</td>
            <td>Sometimes asked</td>
            <td>First night is enough</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Visa or e-visa</td>
            <td>Only if not visa-free</td>
            <td>Print a copy</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Border tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Have internet on landing</strong> so you can open your insurance and bookings — see
          <a href={path("esim-georgia")}>eSIM for Georgia</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Arriving at night?</strong> Book your ride in advance — see
          <a href={path("tbilisi-airport-transfer")}>Tbilisi airport transfer</a>.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Customs:</strong> you can bring personal items, and limited alcohol and tobacco duty-free.</li>
        <li><strong>Overland from Russia:</strong> only the Upper Lars crossing (towards Kazbegi) is legal; never enter via Abkhazia or South Ossetia.</li>
        <li><strong>Overstaying</strong> leads to fines and can lead to a ban, so track your days if you stay long.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      {
        faq.map((f) => (
          <>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }
      <p>
        <em
          >This page is a summary, not legal advice. Rules can change at short notice: check the Georgian Ministry of Foreign
          Affairs consular site (geoconsul.gov.ge) for your passport before travel.</em
        >
      </p>
    </article>

    <aside class="action-panel" data-reveal>
      <h2>Sorted the papers?</h2>
      <p>Next, make sure you land with data and a ride to the city.</p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={aff("esim")} target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored noreferrer"
        >Get a Georgia eSIM</a
      >
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={aff("transfer")} target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored noreferrer"
        >Book airport transfer</a
      >
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={aff("flights")} target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored noreferrer"
        >Compare flights</a
      >
      <p class="disclaimer">Affiliate disclosure: we may earn a commission if you book through these links.</p>
    </aside>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
